<section class="source-band signal-chain-dossier larchive-dossier" aria-labelledby="larchive-layers-heading">
  <div class="section-inner band-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="section-label fi-caption-box"><span class="fi-prompt-marker" aria-hidden="true">&gt;</span>SYSTEM LAYERS</p>
      <h2 id="larchive-layers-heading">THE ARCHIVE HAS THREE INDEPENDENT LAYERS.</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="progression-list">
      <li class="complete">
        <span>I</span>
        <h3>Physical</h3>
        <p>The box, the foam, the object, the identifier. This layer must survive without computers.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>II</span>
        <h3>Digital</h3>
        <p>The index, images, search, version history, and future LE-BOX-000 interface. Useful, but not the archive itself.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="active">
        <span>III</span>
        <h3>Protocol</h3>
        <p>The rules for entry, revision, provenance, deaccession, replicas, and identifier reuse. This is the durable layer.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="evidence-band signal-chain-dossier larchive-dossier" aria-labelledby="larchive-prototype-heading">
  <div class="section-inner band-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="section-label fi-caption-box"><span class="fi-prompt-marker" aria-hidden="true">&gt;</span>V0.1 PROTOTYPE</p>
      <h2 id="larchive-prototype-heading">BUILD THE STORAGE-DISPLAY DEMONSTRATOR FIRST.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="larchive-spec-grid">
      <article>
        <h3>LE-BOX-000</h3>
        <p>Index/control can for v0.1. No Raspberry Pi required yet. Holds the printed register, protocol card, spare labels, revision log, and cartridge map.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>LE-BOX-001 TO 004</h3>
        <p>Four inert archive cartridges. Each holds one object, one provenance card, one removable tray, one viewing pane, and one self-contained light circuit.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>DEFERRED TO V0.2</h3>
        <p>Raspberry Pi, small display, local catalog, and a monochrome menu. The index can become electronic only after the physical interaction is proven.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>SUCCESS TEST</h3>
        <p>Hand someone one cartridge. If they understand one box, one object, one record, and one provenance trail, the prototype works.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="source-band signal-chain-dossier larchive-dossier" aria-labelledby="larchive-cartridge-heading">
  <div class="section-inner band-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="section-label fi-caption-box"><span class="fi-prompt-marker" aria-hidden="true">&gt;</span>CARTRIDGE CONTRACT</p>
      <h2 id="larchive-cartridge-heading">EACH ARCHIVE BOX IS INERT, SWAPPABLE, AND REVERSIBLE.</h2>
    </div>
    <ul class="link-list">
      <li>
        <h3>VIEW</h3>
        <p>Centered top-down window cut into the lid, with clear acrylic or polycarbonate retained underneath. Original hinge and latch remain functional.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>TRAY</h3>
        <p>Removable false-bottom tray resting on brackets. Felt, foam, or archival fabric supports the object without permanent attachment.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>MOUNT</h3>
        <p>Museum wax, thread, or monofilament. No glue on the artifact. The object should leave the cartridge without damage.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>LIGHT</h3>
        <p>CR2032 holder, reed switch, small magnet, LED, and 100 to 220 ohm resistor. The circuit stays inside the cartridge and does not connect to any other box.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="evidence-band signal-chain-dossier larchive-dossier" aria-labelledby="larchive-protocol-heading">
  <div class="section-inner band-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="section-label fi-caption-box"><span class="fi-prompt-marker" aria-hidden="true">&gt;</span>PROTOCOL V0.1</p>
      <h2 id="larchive-protocol-heading">THE LABEL RECORDS WHAT WAS KNOWN AND HOW IT WAS KNOWN.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="larchive-protocol-grid">
      <pre>LE-BOX-003
Protocol: 0.1
Object ID:
Catalogued:
Last Revision:
Status:</pre>
      <ul class="link-list">
        <li>
          <h3>IDENTIFIERS</h3>
          <p>The box ID belongs to the container. The object ID belongs to the artifact. Do not reuse identifiers.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>REVISION</h3>
          <p>If contents, provenance, or status change, the revision log changes with them. The system preserves the evolution of knowledge, not only the final answer.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>STATES</h3>
          <p>RECEIVED / PROVISIONAL / CATALOGUED / PROVENANCE INCOMPLETE / STABLE / DEACCESSIONED.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="source-band signal-chain-dossier larchive-dossier" aria-labelledby="larchive-tub-heading">
  <div class="section-inner band-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="section-label fi-caption-box"><span class="fi-prompt-marker" aria-hidden="true">&gt;</span>CURATOR'S NOTE / 2026-07-13</p>
      <h2 id="larchive-tub-heading">ORIGIN OF LE-TUB.</h2>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="entry-note"><code>LE-TUB</code> entered the protocol during provisional container planning for larger plastic storage units. The first neutral candidate was <code>LE-CONT</code>, short for L'Archive Container. The operator rejected the euphemism.</p>
      <p class="entry-note">Large Tupperware and plastic tubs were not to be disguised as more formal boxes. They could enter custody under their own plain name.</p>
      <ul class="link-list">
        <li>
          <h3>LE-BOX</h3>
          <p>Reserved for the green archive boxes and their cartridge lineage.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>LE-TUB</h3>
          <p>Assigned to larger plastic tub containers when they receive a provisional label, photograph, manifest line, and later revision path.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>CURATORIAL RULE</h3>
          <p>Field language is preserved when it improves retrieval and makes the system harder to lie to.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="source-band signal-chain-dossier larchive-dossier" aria-labelledby="larchive-build-heading">
  <div class="section-inner band-grid">
    <div>
      <p class="section-label fi-caption-box"><span class="fi-prompt-marker" aria-hidden="true">&gt;</span>BUILD ORDER</p>
      <h2 id="larchive-build-heading">ONE BOX FIRST. THEN THE CLUSTER.</h2>
    </div>
    <ol class="progression-list">
      <li class="active">
        <span>01</span>
        <h3>Prototype</h3>
        <p>Build LE-BOX-001 only. Test window size, tray depth, LED placement, label position, and lid operation.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>02</span>
        <h3>Clone</h3>
        <p>Repeat the stable geometry for LE-BOX-002 through LE-BOX-004. Keep window, tray, card, and label positions standardized.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>03</span>
        <h3>Index</h3>
        <p>Build LE-BOX-000 as a printed index can. Use a blank display plate only if it helps preserve the future v0.2 interface position.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>04</span>
        <h3>Document</h3>
        <p>Photograph the finished five-box cluster and file it as the v0.1 reference build.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>05</span>
        <h3>Review</h3>
        <p>Record what failed: latch interference, pane stress, weak light, messy labels, bad tray depth, or unclear status language.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span>06</span>
        <h3>Advance</h3>
        <p>Only after the physical protocol works, advance LE-BOX-000 toward the Raspberry Pi index.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

